","Stimulation of atrial baroreceptors leads to an increase in the activity of neurons in the nucleus tractus solitulus (NTS). This is because atrial baroreceptors are located in the smooth muscle of the atria and respond to changes in blood pressure. When blood pressure increases, the atrial baroreceptors stretch and release norepinephrine, which stimulates the NTS neurons. The NTS neurons then relay this information to the sympathetic and sympathetic nervous systems, which in turn cause a decrease in heart rate and blood pressure.
