"What is the definition of whole body oxygen delivery, and how is it calculated?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
What is the definition of whole body oxygen delivery, and how is it calculated?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The definition of whole body oxygen delivery is the total amount of oxygen that is delivered to the tissues of the body per unit of time. It is an important indicator of the body's ability to deliver oxygen to the tissues and is influenced by factors such as the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood, the rate of blood flow, and the oxygen tension in the blood.

To calculate whole body oxygen delivery, the following formula is used:

VO2 = VO2 × (VO2/V̇E)

where:
VO2 = Whole body oxygen consumption (in mL/min)
VO2 = Oxygen consumption (in mL/min)
V̇E = Volume of expired air (in mL/min)

This formula is based on the Fick equation, which relates the rate of oxygen consumption to the oxygen content of the inspired and expired air. The oxygen content of the inspired air is determined by the barometric pressure and the partial pressure of oxygen in the air. The oxygen content of the expired air is determined by the partial pressure of oxygen in the expired air, which is influenced by the oxygen consumption of the tissues.

In clinical practice, whole body oxygen delivery is often measured using indirect methods, such as the measurement of oxygen consumption (VO2) and the rate of blood flow (V̇E). These measurements can be obtained using various techniques, such as the Douglas method or the Fick equation.
