Calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction in which 3 moles of carbon monoxide (CO) gas is burned with excess oxygen gas to form 3 moles of carbon dioxide (CO2) gas. The reaction is carried out at constant pressure and a temperature of 298 K.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we need to find the difference in the entropy of the products and the reactants. The balanced chemical equation for the reaction is:

2 CO (g) + O2 (g) → 2 CO2 (g)

We can use the standard molar entropies (S°) for each gas to calculate the change in entropy. The standard molar entropies for CO, O2, and CO2 are 186.3 J/mol·K, 205.0 J/mol·K, and 213.7 J/mol·K, respectively.

ΔS = ΣnS°(products) - ΣnS°(reactants)

For the given reaction:

ΔS = [2 × 213.7 J/mol·K] - [2 × 186.3 J/mol·K + 1 × 205.0 J/mol·K]

ΔS = [427.4 J/mol·K] - [372.6 J/mol·K + 205.0 J/mol·K]

ΔS = [427.4 J/mol·K] - [577.6 J/mol·K]

ΔS = -150.2 J/mol·K

Since we have 3 moles of CO reacting, we need to multiply the change in entropy per mole by the number of moles:

ΔS = -150.2 J/mol·K × 3 moles

ΔS = -450.6 J/K

The change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction is -450.6 J/K.
