- The reduction of a metal oxide by carbon depends on the relative reducing power of carbon and the stability of the metal oxide.  
- Aluminum oxide (Al₂O₃) is a very stable oxide due to the high ionization energy of aluminum. Carbon does not have enough reducing power to reduce Al₂O₃ to aluminum.  
- The other oxides (PbO, ZnO, and Fe₂O₃) are more easily reduced by carbon because their metal ions have lower ionization energies.  

