The relationship between vapor pressure and PEL is that substances with higher vapor pressures generally have lower PEL values. This is because substances with higher vapor pressures evaporate more easily, leading to higher concentrations in the air. To maintain a safe exposure level, the PEL must be lower for substances with higher vapor pressures.

In summary, methanol has the highest vapor pressure and the lowest PEL, while ethanol has the lowest vapor pressure and the highest PEL. This relationship is due to the higher volatility of methanol, which requires a lower PEL to ensure safety.
