"In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The key difference between σ (sigma) and π (pi) molecular orbitals lies in the electron density distribution:  
- **σ molecular orbital**: The electron density is concentrated along the axis between the nuclei of the bonded atoms. This is a cylindrically symmetric distribution, meaning the electron density is the same along any axis passing through the internuclear axis.  
- **π molecular orbital**: The electron density is concentrated above and below the axis between the nuclei of the bonded atoms. The electron density is zero along the internuclear axis.  

This difference arises because σ orbitals are formed by the linear combination of atomic orbitals (LCAO) along the internuclear axis, while π orbitals are formed by the LCAO of atomic orbitals perpendicular to the internuclear axis.  

The key difference in electron density distribution leads to differences in bond strength and stability.  
- σ bonds are generally stronger and more stable than π bonds because the electron density is more concentrated between the nuclei, leading to stronger electrostatic attraction.  
- π bonds are weaker and less stable because the electron density is not as concentrated between the nuclei.  

Thus, the key difference is that σ orbitals have electron density along the internuclear axis, while π orbitals have electron density above and below the internuclear axis.  

\boxed{\text{The electron density distribution is the key difference between σ and π molecular orbitals.}}
