","The third law of thermodynamics states that the entropy of a perfectly-ordered crystal approaches zero as the temperature approaches absolute zero. This implies that the entropy of imperfect crystals at absolute zero is also zero, but it is a limit that is never reached in practice. This is because the entropy of an imperfect crystal is higher than that of a perfect crystal due to the presence of defects, such as impurities, dislocations, and vacancies. These defects introduce disorder into the crystal lattice, which increases its entropy.

