","The optimal ratio of titanium dioxide (TiO2) nanoparticles and graphene oxide (GO) in a composite material for use in high-performance dye-sensitized solar cells (DSSCs) is not a fixed value and depends on several factors, such as the specific synthesis method, the size and shape of the nanoparticles, the type of dye used, and the desired properties of the solar cell. However, researchers have found that incorporating GO into TiO2 nanoparticles can improve the overall efficiency and stability of DSSCs.

The optimal ratio of TiO2 to GO typically falls within the range of 90:10 to 70:30, with the highest efficiency being achieved at a ratio of 70:30. This ratio ensures that the TiO2 nanoparticles provide the primary photocatalytic activity and the GO provides additional benefits, such as enhanced charge transport and stability.

The addition of GO to TiO2 nanoparticles in DSSCs can affect the overall efficiency and stability of the solar cell in several ways:

1. Enhanced charge transport: GO has a high electrical conductivity and can facilitate the transport of photogenerated electrons from the TiO2 nanoparticles to the external circuit, thereby improving the overall efficiency of the solar cell.

2. Improved stability: GO can also improve the stability of the solar cell by acting as a barrier to the diffusion of ions and other species that can degrade the TiO2 nanoparticles and the dye molecules.

