","The terms ""isotopologues"" and ""isotopomers"" are used to describe molecules with different isotopic compositions. The key difference lies in the number of atoms that are replaced by isotopes:  
1. **Isotopologues**: These are molecules that differ only in the isotopic composition of one or more atoms. For example, \(^{12}\text{C}_2\text{H}_4\) and \(^{13}\text{C}_2\text{H}_4\) are isotopologues because they differ only in the isotopic composition of one carbon atom.  
2. **Isotopomers**: These are molecules that differ only in the isotopic composition of all of their atoms. For example, \(^{12}\text{C}_2\text{H}_4\) and \(^{13}\text{C}_2^{37}\text{C}_2\text{H}_4\) are isotopomers because all of their atoms are replaced by isotopes.  

Thus, **isotopoplogues** are a broader term, while **isotopomers** are a more specific case of isotopologues.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Isopologues: Different isotopic composition of one or more atoms.} \\
&\text{Isotomers: Different isotopic composition of all atoms.}
\end{aligned}
}
