","The recurrence relation for the number of comparisons in the best-case case of QuickScreen is given by:
\[
T(n) = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } n = 1, \\
T(n/2) + T(n/2) + n - 1 & \text{if } n > 1.
\end{cases}
\]
This is derived as follows:
1. **Base Case**: If \( n = 1 \), the input set has only one element, so no comparisons are needed. Thus, \( T(1) = 0 \).
2. **Main Case**: For \( n > 1 \), the pivot element splits the input set into two subsets of size \( n/2 \).
   - The number of comparisons required to process each subset is \( T(n/2) \).
   - The pivot element is compared to all \( n - 1 \) other elements to determine its position.
   - Thus, the total number of comparisons is \( T(n/2) + T(n/2) + n - 1 \).

This recurrence is derived from the fact that the number of comparisons in the best-case case of QuickScreen is the sum of the number of comparisons required to process the two subsets plus the number of comparisons required to place the pivot element.
