</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The decay length \(L_d\) of the evanescent wave is given by:
\[
L_d = \frac{\pi}{\lambda} \left(n_{\text{eff}}^2 - n_f^2\right)^{-1/2}
\]
where \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective refractive index, \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the cladding, and \(\lambda\) is the wavelength.

Given \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\) and \(\lambda = 1 \mu m = 10^{-6} \, \text{m}\), we can estimate the decay length:
\[
L_d \approx \frac{\pi}{10^{-6} \times 0.3} = \frac{3.14}{3 \times 10^{-7}} \approx 1.04 \times 10^6 \, \text{m} = 1.04 \, \text{km}
\]

Thus, the decay length is approximately \(\boxed{1.04 \, \text{km}}\).

