The most commonly used method for measuring \( \alpha_s \) is through the **deep-angle Compton scattering** (DACS) process, where a high-energy photon scatters with a nucleon. The cross section for this process is highly sensitive to \( \alpha_s \), and its measurement allows for a precise determination of the beta function.

Another method is the **measurement of the quark mass renormalization constants**, which are also sensitive to \( \alpha_s \). This method is particularly useful for determining \( \alpha_s \) at low energy scales.

In summary, the most precise method for measuring \( \alpha_s \) is through the measurement of the beta function and its derivatives, with DACS and quark mass renormalization constants being the most commonly used methods.
