In a Cartesian coordinate system, what is the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) that depends only on \(x\) and \(y\)? Express the result in terms of its components.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To find the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) in Cartesian coordinates, we use the following formula:

\[
\mathbf{L} = \nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ \frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\ V_x & V_y & V_z \end{vmatrix}
\]

Given that \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) depends only on \(x\) and \(y\), the \(z\)-component \(V_z = 0\). The curl simplifies to:

\[
\mathbf{L} = \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ \frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & 0 \\ V_x & V_y & 0 \end{vmatrix}
\]

Expanding the determinant:

\[
\mathbf{L} = \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \cdot 0 - 0 \cdot \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{i} - \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \cdot 0 - 0 \cdot \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \cdot V_y - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \cdot V_x \right) \mathbf{k}
\]

Simplifying further:

\[
\mathbf{L} = \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
\]

Thus, the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) that depends only on \(x\) and \(y\) is:

\[
\mathbf{L} = \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
\]

or in terms of the curl operator \(\nabla \times\):

\[
\mathbf{L} = \nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
\]

This result is consistent with the fact that the curl of a vector field represents the circulation of the field around a point. If the field depends only on \(x\) and \(y\), there is no rotation in the \(z\)-direction, so the curl is only non-zero in the \(z\)-direction. The magnitude of the curl is given by the difference between the partial derivatives of the \(y\)- and \(x\)-components of the field.

</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

