Calculate the activation energy \( E_a \) for the reaction \( H_2 + I_2 \rightarrow 2HI \) using the Arrhenius equation. Given the forward rate constants \( k_{f1} = 10^{-1} \) at \( 1/T_1 = 0.0018 \, \text{K}^{-1} \) and \( k_{f2} = 10^4 \) at \( 1/T_2 = 0.0012 \, \text{K}^{-1} \), and the gas constant \( R = 2 \, \text{cal K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1} \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the activation energy \( E_a \), we use the Arrhenius equation:

\[
k = A e^{-E_a / RT}
\]

Taking the natural logarithm of both sides:

\[
\ln k = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT}
\]

Taking the difference in the natural logarithm of the rate constants at two temperatures:

\[
\ln \left( \frac{k_2}{k_1} \right) = \frac{E_a}{R} \left( \frac{1}{T_1} - \frac{1}{T_2} \right)
\]

Rearranging to solve for \( E_a \):

\[
E_a = \frac{\ln \left( \frac{k_2}{k_1} \right)}{\frac{1}{T_1} - \frac{1}{T_2}} \cdot R
\]

Substitute the given values:

\[
E_a = \frac{\ln \left( \frac{10^4}{10^{-1}} \right)}{\frac{0.0018}{1} - \frac{0.0012}{1}} \cdot 2
\]

Calculate the numerator:

\[
\ln \left( \frac{10^4}{10^{-1}} \right) = \ln (10^5) = 5 \ln 10
\]

Now compute the denominator:

\[
\frac{0.0018}{1} - \frac{0.0012}{1} = 0.0006 \, \text{K}^{-1}
\]

Substitute back into the equation:

\[
E_a = \frac{5 \ln 10}{0.0006} \cdot 2
\]

Calculate the final result:

\[
E_a = \frac{5 \times 2.3026}{0.0006} \cdot 2 = 77.42 \, \text{cal K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1}
\]

Thus, the activation energy for the reaction is \(\boxed{77.42 \, \text{cal K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1}}\).
