For the proton and neutron, the quark combinations are as follows:

Proton: u, d, u (one up quark and two down quarks)
Neutron: u, d, u (one up quark and two down quarks)

The spin wave function of the proton and neutron is constructed by considering the spin of each quark and the Pauli exclusion principle. The Pauli exclusion principle states that no two identical fermions can occupy the same quantum state. In the case of the proton and neutron, the three quarks are identical fermions, so their spin wave functions must be antisymmetric. The spin wave function can be constructed as a linear combination of the spin states of the three quarks, ensuring antisymmetry.

To construct the spin wave function, we can use the Young representation of the SU(3) group, which describes the symmetry of the quark system. The proton and neutron are in the isospplet representation of the SU(3) group, which means that their spin wave functions can be written as a linear combination of the triplet and singlet states of the isospin operator.

